  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 9,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.584,
            0.027,
            0.805,
            0.043
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 5 — Exploring Forces"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.083,
            0.584,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We found that the two similarly charged balloons repel each other whereas a charged balloon and the woollen cloth (with which the balloon was rubbed) attract each other. Does"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.601,
            0.086,
            0.803,
            0.141
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Does it mean that there are two kinds of electrical charges?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.836,
            0.069,
            0.919,
            0.179
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.836,
                0.069,
                0.919,
                0.179
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_9_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.157,
            0.744,
            0.195
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "this "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "indicate",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " that the charge on the balloon is of a different kind from the charge on the woollen cloth?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.197,
            0.744,
            0.345
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Since the balloons were charged in the same way, we can say that they have acquired similar charges. As the similarly charged balloons repelled each other, we can infer that similar (like) charges repel each other. Both the rubbing object and the rubbed object get charged but they acquire opposite kind of charges. Their attraction shows that opposite kind (unlike) of charges attract each other. The two kinds of static charges are said to be ‘positive’ and ‘negative’."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.345,
            0.743,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The force exerted by a charged body on another charged body or an uncharged body is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "electrostatic force",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". It is a non-contact force."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.41,
            0.31,
            0.43
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A step further"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.447,
            0.722,
            0.502
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When the charges move, they constitute an electric current in an electrical circuit. It is the same current which makes a lamp glow "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "or generates a heating effect or a magnetic effect.",
              "styles": [
                "underline"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.739,
            0.378,
            0.908,
            0.503
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.739,
                0.378,
                0.908,
                0.503
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_9_image_9.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.516,
            0.338,
            0.535
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Gravitational force"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.549,
            0.488,
            0.573
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 5.8: Let us observe"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.593,
            0.743,
            0.63
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Take a ball and throw it vertically upwards. Does it come down?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.635,
            0.742,
            0.673
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Now throw it again, but this time harder. Does it still fall back down to the ground?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.678,
            0.744,
            0.734
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think about different situations around you where any object thrown up in any direction, finally falls or comes back to the ground or floor (Fig. 5.10). Me"
            }
          ]
        },
